      "text": "You are comparing two texts written in response to the same task. Decide which one reads MORE like unedited, default output from an AI language model.\n\nJudge ONLY the presence of the tells listed below. Do not judge argument quality, factual accuracy, length, formatting preference, or whether you agree with the content. A longer, better-argued or more polished text is not more human. Pick the text with MORE tells.\n\nTELLS OF UNEDITED AI PROSE (judge on these only)\n\nStructure\n1. Negative parallelism used as a device: \"It's not X. It's Y.\" / \"not just X, but Y\" / \"isn't about X, it's about Y\", especially more than once.\n2. Signposting: the text announces what it is about to do (\"Let's break this down\", \"Here's the thing\", \"Here's why\", \"The bottom line\", \"The takeaway\").\n3. Self-applause: \"Let that sink in\", \"This changes everything\", \"Read that again\", \"Make no mistake\".\n4. Recap endings: a closing paragraph that restates the body (\"In conclusion\", \"Ultimately\", \"At the end of the day\", \"In short\").\n5. Bold-label paragraph openers (\"**The problem.** ...\") and headers, bullets or numbered lists where prose was asked for.\n6. Triads everywhere: lists of exactly three items or three adjectives, sentence after sentence.\n7. Participial tails: sentences ending in \", making it ...\", \", ensuring that ...\", \", allowing teams to ...\".\n8. Invented labels for ordinary observations (\"I call this the Clarity Flywheel\").\n9. Uniform rhythm: every sentence about the same length, or relentless short punchy fragments. Real writing has one sentence that runs on and one short one.\n10. Rhetorical question followed by its own answer (\"The result? Nobody used it.\").\n\nWords\n11. Overused vocabulary: delve, tapestry, myriad, underscore, seamless, robust, foster, elevate, unlock, testament, landscape, navigate, crucial, pivotal, leverage, harness, realm, embark, journey, transformative, paradigm, ever-evolving, multifaceted, holistic, resonate, empower, unleash, beacon, intricate, nuanced, vibrant, meticulous, streamline, game-changer, cutting-edge, actionable, supercharge, north star, move the needle, \"in today's fast-paced world\", \"it's worth noting\", \"plays a crucial role\".\n12. Filler transitions at sentence start: However, Additionally, Furthermore, Moreover, That said, Ultimately, Importantly, Indeed, Of course.\n13. Em dashes used as the default joint between clauses.\n14. Artificial enthusiasm: \"thrilled to announce\", \"excited to share\", \"we've got you covered\", \"say goodbye to\", \"buckle up\".\n\nReader\n15. Performing for an imagined crowd rather than talking to one person; nothing specific (no number, name, date, or concrete detail) for several sentences in a row.\n16. Generic or manufactured anecdotes: a story with no specific details that could only be true of one person.\n17. Every point lands, every ending uplifts; the author is never irritated, uncertain, or wrong about anything.\n18. Explaining what the reader could infer from the previous sentence (\"what this means is ...\").\n\n\nTASK GIVEN TO BOTH WRITERS:\nWrite a launch announcement of up to 500 words for a new feature of a B2B helpdesk product: a Slack integration that posts a daily summary of open support tickets to a channel of the customer's choice. The audience is existing customers.\n\n=== TEXT A ===\n**Subject: Now live — your daily open-ticket summary, delivered right to Slack**\n\nHi there,\n\nQuick question: how many times a day does someone on your team open the helpdesk dashboard just to check how many tickets are open? If the answer is \"too many,\" this one's for you.\n\nWe're excited to announce our new Slack integration: a daily summary of your open support tickets, posted automatically to the Slack channel of your choice.\n\n**What you'll get**\n\nEvery day, at a time you choose, we'll post a clean, digestible summary straight to your team's channel:\n\n- **Total open tickets**, with day-over-day change\n- **New tickets** opened in the last 24 hours\n- **At-risk tickets** approaching or breaching SLA\n- **Aging tickets** — your oldest unresolved issues, so nothing slips through the cracks\n- **A breakdown by priority, queue, or assignee**\n\nEach ticket links directly back to the helpdesk, so anyone who needs to dig in can get there in one click.\n\n**Why we built it**\n\nSupport doesn't happen in a vacuum. Managers want a daily pulse check without running reports. Colleagues in other departments want to know what's coming without asking. And your team leads want at-risk tickets surfaced *before* they become escalations — not after.\n\nNow everyone can start the day with the same picture of what's open, what's urgent, and what needs attention — without anyone leaving Slack.\n\n**Getting started takes about two minutes**\n\n1. In your helpdesk, go to **Settings → Integrations → Slack** and connect your workspace (no new logins required).\n2. Choose the channel where you'd like the summary posted — #support-ops, #leadership, wherever it's most useful.\n3. Pick your posting time. Most teams choose first thing in the morning.\n4. Save. Your first digest arrives tomorrow.\n\n**A few things worth knowing**\n\n- The integration is available on **all plans at no additional cost**.\n- Summaries only post to channels your Slack admin permits, and only channel members can see them — no ticket data is shared beyond what you choose.\n- You can change your channel, timing, or digest contents anytime in Settings.\n\n**Try it today**\n\nSet up your first digest now: **[Enable the Slack Integration]**\n\nQuestions? Your customer success manager is happy to help — or just reply to this email. A human will read it (we use our own helpdesk, after all).\n\nHere's to fewer dashboard checks and clearer mornings,\n\nThe [Product] Team\n=== END OF TEXT A ===\n\n=== TEXT B ===\n**New Feature Launch: Daily Open Ticket Summaries in Slack**\n\nWe’re introducing a new Slack integration that keeps your team aligned with a daily summary of open support tickets—delivered directly to a Slack channel of your choice.\n\nNow available for existing customers, this feature makes it easier to see what’s on your support team’s plate without logging into the helpdesk. Each day, you’ll receive a clear, concise update highlighting open tickets so managers, agents, and cross-functional stakeholders can prioritize work, spot trends, and stay proactive.\n\nInstead of chasing status updates or digging through queues, your team can start each day with the same shared view of support activity. Whether you use Slack for daily standups, team updates, or operational reviews, these summaries help keep everyone focused on the customer conversations that need attention next.\n\n**What you’ll see in your daily summary**\n\n- Total open support tickets  \n- Tickets that may need priority attention  \n- A quick overview of workload across your team or queue  \n- A simple way to stay up to date without switching tools\n\n**Why it matters**\n\nSupport teams often manage competing priorities across email, chat, phone, and escalations. This integration reduces manual checking and keeps critical ticket information visible to the right people at the right time. It supports faster triage, better collaboration, and stronger daily planning—especially for teams managing high ticket volumes or shared accountability across departments.\n\n**How to get started**\n\nConnecting the integration is simple. Choose the Slack channel where you want the daily summary to appear, select the ticket queue or view you want to track, and enable the update. From there, the summary will be posted automatically each day.\n\nIf you’d like help setting it up, our support team can guide you through the steps.\n\nWith this launch, we’re making it easier for your team to stay informed, organized, and ready to support your customers—without leaving Slack.\n=== END OF TEXT B ===\n\nAnswer with